$g(x)$ is the quotient of two, more basic, expressions: $\sin(x)$ and $e^x$.

Therefore, the derivative of $g$ can be found using **the quotient rule**:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}\right]&=\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}[u(x)]v(x)-u(x)\dfrac{d}{dx}[v(x)]}{[v(x)]^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}
\end{align}$

Let's differentiate!

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}g'(x)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{\sin(x)}{e^x}\right)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}(\sin(x))e^x-\sin(x)\dfrac{d}{dx}(e^x)}{(e^x)^2}&&\gray{\text{The quotient rule}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cos(x)\cdot e^x-\sin(x)\cdot e^x}{(e^x)^2}&&\gray{\text{Differentiate }\sin(x)\text{ and }e^x}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{e^x}(\cos(x)-\sin(x))}{(e^x)^\cancel2}&&\gray{\text{Simplify}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cos(x)-\sin(x)}{e^x}
\end{align}$
